1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:04,842
Hello, and welcome to the DigiTech submission
for the Digital Twin Challenge twenty twenty-six.

2
00:00:04,910 --> 00:00:06,985
Our project is called GlucoTwin India.

3
00:00:07,053 --> 00:00:11,203
It is a focused, patient-specific digital
twin proof of concept designed to

4
00:00:11,270 --> 00:00:15,421
estimate the likelihood of a synthetic
glucose reading crossing a chosen threshold

5
00:00:15,488 --> 00:00:17,218
within the next two hours.

6
00:00:17,285 --> 00:00:21,437
The idea is to combine historical patient
context with a dynamic stream

7
00:00:21,504 --> 00:00:26,346
resembling continuous glucose monitoring and wearable measurements,
rather than treating each reading in isolation.

8
00:00:26,413 --> 00:00:29,872
We chose one carefully defined use case
because a complete,

9
00:00:29,940 --> 00:00:34,090
whole-body digital twin is beyond the
scope of a short student challenge.

10
00:00:34,158 --> 00:00:37,617
We want the reviewer to see the entire
engineering loop:

11
00:00:37,684 --> 00:00:41,489
generation of a patient profile, time-aligned
sensor-like measurements, a machine-learning forecast,

12
00:00:41,556 --> 00:00:46,053
an interface for human review, and an
explicit explanation of the system's limitations.

13
00:00:46,121 --> 00:00:49,580
Every patient and every measurement in
this prototype is synthetic.

14
00:00:49,647 --> 00:00:53,106
This presentation is a research
demonstration, not a medical device,

15
00:00:53,173 --> 00:00:57,324
and no output should be used to diagnose a
person, select treatment,

16
00:00:57,391 --> 00:00:59,812
change medication, or make an emergency
decision.

17
00:00:59,880 --> 00:01:02,647
I will walk through the problem, model,
results,

18
00:01:02,714 --> 00:01:04,402
user experience and next steps.

19
00:01:04,444 --> 00:01:07,143
Diabetes is a major public-health issue in
India.

20
00:01:07,210 --> 00:01:10,584
The International Diabetes Federation's
eleventh edition of the Diabetes Atlas,

21
00:01:10,651 --> 00:01:14,699
published in twenty twenty-five, estimates that
approximately ninety million adults aged twenty

22
00:01:14,766 --> 00:01:18,478
to seventy-nine were living with diabetes
in India in twenty twenty-four.

23
00:01:18,545 --> 00:01:22,593
This number is an estimate based on the
Atlas's definitions and methodology;

24
00:01:22,661 --> 00:01:26,709
it should not be confused with a live
count of people who

25
00:01:26,777 --> 00:01:28,126
have received a diagnosis.

26
00:01:28,193 --> 00:01:32,242
The World Health Organization also
describes diabetes as a condition that can

27
00:01:32,309 --> 00:01:37,032
lead to serious long-term complications and emphasises
the importance of appropriate detection and management.

28
00:01:37,099 --> 00:01:40,473
Our project does not claim to solve
diabetes, diagnose diabetes,

29
00:01:40,541 --> 00:01:42,228
or predict every possible complication.

30
00:01:42,295 --> 00:01:46,343
Instead, it asks a small engineering
question: can a patient-specific data view

31
00:01:46,410 --> 00:01:51,134
combine a historical profile with current context
and estimate a clearly defined short-term event?

32
00:01:51,201 --> 00:01:55,250
A single laboratory value is useful but
cannot describe every change in

33
00:01:55,317 --> 00:01:58,016
activity, meals, sleep and glucose over a
day.

34
00:01:58,084 --> 00:02:02,131
A continuous data stream offers more
context, but it can be difficult

35
00:02:02,199 --> 00:02:05,572
to review manually when signals are spread
across several sources.

36
00:02:05,640 --> 00:02:09,688
A digital twin prototype offers a way to
join those sources around

37
00:02:09,756 --> 00:02:11,105
one evolving patient state.

38
00:02:11,173 --> 00:02:14,884
We use this public-health context to
explain why the area matters;

39
00:02:14,951 --> 00:02:17,987
the public-health statistic is not used as
model-training data.

40
00:02:18,054 --> 00:02:22,103
Another design choice is to avoid a broad
claim such as predicting

41
00:02:22,170 --> 00:02:25,881
a heart attack, a hospital admission or an
overall diabetes outcome.

42
00:02:25,948 --> 00:02:31,009
Those would require much richer outcome definitions and
stronger evidence than this prototype can provide.

43
00:02:31,076 --> 00:02:34,113
By choosing a short-term glucose
excursion, the input fields,

44
00:02:34,180 --> 00:02:38,566
prediction horizon and retrospective label are easy
for the evaluation panel to inspect.

45
00:02:38,633 --> 00:02:41,332
The focus is on an end-to-end technical
proof,

46
00:02:41,400 --> 00:02:45,068
not on claiming a clinical benefit before
it has been tested.

47
00:02:45,111 --> 00:02:46,663
The target is deliberately narrow.

48
00:02:46,731 --> 00:02:50,147
At an observation time, the simulator
records a current glucose-like value.

49
00:02:50,215 --> 00:02:53,941
If that current value is below one hundred
and eighty milligrams per

50
00:02:54,009 --> 00:02:57,736
decilitre, we ask whether either of the
next two hourly simulated readings

51
00:02:57,803 --> 00:02:59,977
reaches or exceeds one hundred and eighty.

52
00:03:00,045 --> 00:03:02,529
If it does, the synthetic label is
positive;

53
00:03:02,596 --> 00:03:04,150
otherwise the label is negative.

54
00:03:04,217 --> 00:03:07,322
We chose this definition so the prediction
horizon is clear

55
00:03:07,390 --> 00:03:10,806
and can be tested against an outcome
generated by the simulator.

56
00:03:10,874 --> 00:03:13,048
There is an important safety distinction
here.

57
00:03:13,115 --> 00:03:16,842
When the current simulated value is
already at or above the threshold,

58
00:03:16,910 --> 00:03:19,394
the row is excluded from the early-warning
model.

59
00:03:19,461 --> 00:03:22,256
The dashboard identifies that as a current
elevated reading

60
00:03:22,324 --> 00:03:26,361
rather than pretending that the model predicted
an event that is already present.

61
00:03:26,429 --> 00:03:29,845
The threshold is an artificial label for
this proof of concept,

62
00:03:29,913 --> 00:03:31,776
not a universal clinical alert rule.

63
00:03:31,844 --> 00:03:35,570
In real care, appropriate glucose targets
and the meaning of a reading

64
00:03:35,638 --> 00:03:38,433
depend on clinical context and a qualified
clinician's judgment.

65
00:03:38,500 --> 00:03:41,606
The system outputs a model estimate, not a
medical instruction.

66
00:03:41,674 --> 00:03:45,710
A low model score also cannot guarantee
that an event will not happen.

67
00:03:45,778 --> 00:03:49,815
We make these distinctions visible in the
interface and in the repository documentation.

68
00:03:49,883 --> 00:03:52,678
The model output is shown as a
probability-like score,

69
00:03:52,745 --> 00:03:55,851
but probability estimates only deserve
confidence when they are tested

70
00:03:55,919 --> 00:03:59,024
and calibrated in the setting where they
will be used.

71
00:03:59,092 --> 00:04:03,440
In this repository, the model is trained
and tested against a generated data process.

72
00:04:03,507 --> 00:04:07,235
The numerical score is therefore best
understood as the model estimate within

73
00:04:07,302 --> 00:04:10,718
that process, not as an absolute statement
about a real person.

74
00:04:10,785 --> 00:04:14,512
The event definition is explicit so that
another developer can reproduce the

75
00:04:14,579 --> 00:04:18,575
label, question our assumptions, or change
the simulator and run a new experiment.

76
00:04:18,617 --> 00:04:22,870
The first input stream is historical and
resembles an electronic health record.

77
00:04:22,938 --> 00:04:26,483
It includes synthetic age, sex, body-mass
index, years since diagnosis,

78
00:04:26,550 --> 00:04:30,449
HbA one C, a hypertension flag and a
simulated medication-adherence factor.

79
00:04:30,517 --> 00:04:35,125
These values represent background context and stay
relatively stable across the patient's timeline.

80
00:04:35,192 --> 00:04:39,445
They are not taken from an actual person
and the simulated adherence

81
00:04:39,513 --> 00:04:41,994
value is not a recommendation about
medication.

82
00:04:42,061 --> 00:04:44,188
The second input stream changes hourly.

83
00:04:44,256 --> 00:04:47,446
It contains the current glucose-like
value, its recent change,

84
00:04:47,513 --> 00:04:51,058
a short-window mean and variability, the
latest meal carbohydrate log,

85
00:04:51,125 --> 00:04:55,379
recent step count, heart rate, heart-rate
variability, previous-night sleep duration, sleep efficiency,

86
00:04:55,446 --> 00:04:58,991
time since a recorded meal and a simple
stress proxy.

87
00:04:59,058 --> 00:05:03,311
These data emulate the kinds of signals
that could be produced by

88
00:05:03,379 --> 00:05:06,569
a glucose sensor, a wearable and a meal
diary.

89
00:05:06,636 --> 00:05:10,890
The two streams are joined by a synthetic
patient identifier and timestamp.

90
00:05:10,957 --> 00:05:16,274
The feature vector at a selected time only
uses the information available at that time.

91
00:05:16,342 --> 00:05:20,240
Future glucose is retained separately to
create the retrospective outcome label,

92
00:05:20,308 --> 00:05:24,562
but it is deliberately excluded from the
feature list used for prediction.

93
00:05:24,629 --> 00:05:28,174
This separation is necessary for a fair
and understandable demonstration.

94
00:05:28,241 --> 00:05:32,495
The signals are intentionally diverse
because a digital twin should not simply

95
00:05:32,562 --> 00:05:36,107
duplicate one sensor column and rename it
as artificial intelligence.

96
00:05:36,174 --> 00:05:41,491
The historical profile can provide baseline context, while
recent dynamics can show a short-lived change.

97
00:05:41,558 --> 00:05:45,458
The model is not given a patient's
identifier as a feature,

98
00:05:45,525 --> 00:05:49,778
so it cannot learn an arbitrary
patient-specific shortcut by memorising an identifier.

99
00:05:49,846 --> 00:05:54,454
The identifier is used for joining records
and for forming patient-level evaluation partitions.

100
00:05:54,521 --> 00:05:58,066
In the dashboard, the reviewer sees both
streams presented together

101
00:05:58,133 --> 00:06:00,927
rather than needing to manually reconcile
multiple tables.

102
00:06:00,969 --> 00:06:03,021
The architecture has six main stages.

103
00:06:03,088 --> 00:06:06,850
First, a generator creates synthetic patient
profiles with historical EHR-like factors.

104
00:06:06,918 --> 00:06:11,365
Second, it simulates a sequence of hourly
sensor-like values and a glucose trajectory.

105
00:06:11,432 --> 00:06:16,220
Third, a feature pipeline builds a time-aligned
observation using only current and past signals.

106
00:06:16,288 --> 00:06:20,734
Fourth, an Extra Trees classifier estimates the
chance of the defined two-hour outcome.

107
00:06:20,801 --> 00:06:24,906
Fifth, patient-level evaluation measures how the
model performs on held-out synthetic patients.

108
00:06:24,973 --> 00:06:28,394
Sixth, a Streamlit dashboard displays the
current reading, historical context,

109
00:06:28,461 --> 00:06:31,539
the recent trend, the forecast and a
review cue.

110
00:06:31,607 --> 00:06:34,343
The interface does not trigger an
automated action.

111
00:06:34,410 --> 00:06:37,831
It asks a human reviewer to verify the
data stream,

112
00:06:37,898 --> 00:06:42,002
look at the recent context and decide
whether any follow-up is appropriate.

113
00:06:42,069 --> 00:06:46,175
The architecture also includes a safety
layer: no live EHR is connected,

114
00:06:46,242 --> 00:06:49,662
no wearable is connected, no real health
data is collected,

115
00:06:49,729 --> 00:06:53,150
and the future outcome is not available to
inference-time features.

116
00:06:53,217 --> 00:06:56,295
Every score should be interpreted within
the simulated environment.

117
00:06:56,363 --> 00:07:00,468
The included architecture diagram is available
as a PDF, a high-resolution image,

118
00:07:00,535 --> 00:07:03,271
and a figure inside the editable
PowerPoint deck,

119
00:07:03,338 --> 00:07:07,101
so the workflow can be inspected without
relying only on narration.

120
00:07:07,168 --> 00:07:10,930
Each stage can be traced to a file in the
repository.

121
00:07:10,998 --> 00:07:14,076
Data generation is implemented in the
synthetic data module,

122
00:07:14,143 --> 00:07:17,222
feature names are centrally listed in one
feature-contract module,

123
00:07:17,289 --> 00:07:20,368
training and evaluation are handled in the
training module,

124
00:07:20,435 --> 00:07:23,171
and the dashboard calls a separate
prediction helper.

125
00:07:23,238 --> 00:07:27,343
That separation is useful for maintenance
and also makes it easier for

126
00:07:27,410 --> 00:07:31,514
a reviewer to check whether future
information accidentally enters the model inputs.

127
00:07:31,582 --> 00:07:35,345
The architecture is simple by design: the
goal is a working,

128
00:07:35,412 --> 00:07:39,516
understandable pipeline that can be improved,
rather than a collection of disconnected

129
00:07:39,584 --> 00:07:41,594
components that is difficult to audit.

130
00:07:41,636 --> 00:07:45,790
The repository contains eighty thousand one
hundred and sixty hourly records generated

131
00:07:45,857 --> 00:07:48,627
for two hundred and forty artificial
patient profiles.

132
00:07:48,694 --> 00:07:51,810
Each patient has approximately fourteen
days of hourly observations.

133
00:07:51,878 --> 00:07:54,993
The simulator introduces variation in
demographics and historical factors,

134
00:07:55,060 --> 00:07:58,869
then generates hourly meal events, step
counts, heart rate, heart-rate variability,

135
00:07:58,936 --> 00:08:00,667
sleep context and glucose-like readings.

136
00:08:00,735 --> 00:08:04,888
The glucose trajectory follows a stochastic
recurrence influenced by the patient's synthetic

137
00:08:04,956 --> 00:08:09,111
baseline, recent meal log, activity, adherence
factor, stress proxy and random noise.

138
00:08:09,178 --> 00:08:13,332
This is not a clinical simulator and does
not reproduce the full

139
00:08:13,399 --> 00:08:14,784
biology of glucose regulation.

140
00:08:14,852 --> 00:08:18,314
It is a controlled data generator for a
programming challenge.

141
00:08:18,381 --> 00:08:21,496
The benefit is reproducibility: the source
code is visible,

142
00:08:21,564 --> 00:08:25,718
the default seed is fixed, and the same
pipeline can be run

143
00:08:25,786 --> 00:08:27,516
again to regenerate the dataset.

144
00:08:27,584 --> 00:08:30,700
The model cannot learn from a hidden
clinical database

145
00:08:30,767 --> 00:08:32,844
because no clinical database is included.

146
00:08:32,912 --> 00:08:36,719
The target label is created from the next
two simulated readings,

147
00:08:36,787 --> 00:08:41,634
which makes it possible to verify whether
the model's prediction matches the artificial outcome.

148
00:08:41,701 --> 00:08:44,124
A separate data dictionary explains each
column.

149
00:08:44,192 --> 00:08:48,000
The repository includes both the generated
records and the generation code

150
00:08:48,067 --> 00:08:51,183
so reviewers can see where the values come
from

151
00:08:51,251 --> 00:08:52,981
and what assumptions underlie them.

152
00:08:53,049 --> 00:08:57,203
Synthetic generation also lets the evaluator
run the application without a credential,

153
00:08:57,271 --> 00:09:00,386
hospital affiliation or access to a
restricted health database.

154
00:09:00,453 --> 00:09:03,569
The data includes artificial sensor noise
and random variation

155
00:09:03,637 --> 00:09:08,483
so the same profile does not produce an
identical flat line at every hour.

156
00:09:08,550 --> 00:09:12,705
However, these random effects are still
the assumptions of our own simulator.

157
00:09:12,772 --> 00:09:16,926
A model trained on this data cannot be
described as robust simply

158
00:09:16,994 --> 00:09:18,725
because the simulator includes noise.

159
00:09:18,792 --> 00:09:22,947
A subsequent experiment would need to test
the model on a distinct

160
00:09:23,014 --> 00:09:26,822
and appropriately governed source, and then
determine whether the input definitions

161
00:09:26,889 --> 00:09:28,578
and alert threshold remain meaningful.

162
00:09:28,621 --> 00:09:31,352
The chosen baseline is an Extra Trees
classifier.

163
00:09:31,420 --> 00:09:34,834
It is appropriate for a small tabular
proof of concept

164
00:09:34,902 --> 00:09:37,975
because it can capture nonlinear
relationships between patient context

165
00:09:38,042 --> 00:09:41,456
and recent time-series summaries without
requiring a large compute environment.

166
00:09:41,524 --> 00:09:44,939
The model uses nineteen features,
including historical factors, current glucose,

167
00:09:45,006 --> 00:09:48,420
recent glucose statistics, meal logs,
activity, heart rate, heart-rate variability,

168
00:09:48,488 --> 00:09:50,195
sleep and a stress proxy.

169
00:09:50,263 --> 00:09:54,018
Future glucose and the outcome label are
not among those features.

170
00:09:54,086 --> 00:09:56,817
We divide the dataset by unique patient
IDs,

171
00:09:56,885 --> 00:09:58,592
not by randomly separating rows.

172
00:09:58,660 --> 00:10:01,733
One hundred and fifty-three patients are
used for training,

173
00:10:01,800 --> 00:10:04,531
thirty-nine for validation and forty-eight
for final testing.

174
00:10:04,599 --> 00:10:08,696
That means one patient's hourly measurements
do not appear in both training

175
00:10:08,763 --> 00:10:11,837
and test data, reducing an important
source of leakage.

176
00:10:11,904 --> 00:10:16,001
We choose the decision threshold on the
validation set by maximising the

177
00:10:16,069 --> 00:10:20,849
F two score, which weights recall more
heavily than precision for this early-warning demonstration.

178
00:10:20,917 --> 00:10:25,355
Because positive events are rare, the
threshold is much lower than one half.

179
00:10:25,423 --> 00:10:30,545
This does not mean that every score above
it should be treated as an emergency.

180
00:10:30,612 --> 00:10:34,027
It is an operating point for a synthetic
research demo

181
00:10:34,094 --> 00:10:35,801
and should be treated cautiously.

182
00:10:35,869 --> 00:10:39,284
The report contains the actual partition
sizes, feature list, threshold,

183
00:10:39,351 --> 00:10:41,058
confusion matrix and score metrics.

184
00:10:41,125 --> 00:10:45,223
We also keep a held-out predictions file
so the reader can compare

185
00:10:45,290 --> 00:10:49,046
the forecast score with the corresponding
artificial label for test records.

186
00:10:49,114 --> 00:10:53,211
That makes error analysis possible: false
positives can be reviewed to understand

187
00:10:53,278 --> 00:10:56,693
which simulator situations create excess
alerts, while false negatives show

188
00:10:56,760 --> 00:10:58,809
which events the selected threshold
misses.

189
00:10:58,876 --> 00:11:02,974
It is not enough to report overall
accuracy when the positive event

190
00:11:03,041 --> 00:11:07,139
is uncommon, because a model can look
accurate while missing important events.

191
00:11:07,206 --> 00:11:11,303
Here we report ranking metrics and
threshold-based metrics together to give a

192
00:11:11,371 --> 00:11:13,036
more balanced picture of performance.

193
00:11:13,078 --> 00:11:16,833
The final test set contains measurements
from forty-eight held-out synthetic patients.

194
00:11:16,900 --> 00:11:20,997
On that set, the area under the receiver
operating characteristic curve is

195
00:11:21,064 --> 00:11:25,160
approximately zero point nine zero two,
and average precision is approximately zero

196
00:11:25,227 --> 00:11:26,593
point three three eight.

197
00:11:26,660 --> 00:11:29,733
At the validation-selected threshold of
zero point one one,

198
00:11:29,800 --> 00:11:33,897
recall is approximately zero point six
eight five and precision is approximately

199
00:11:33,964 --> 00:11:35,671
zero point two five nine.

200
00:11:35,738 --> 00:11:39,151
The Brier score is approximately zero
point zero four one.

201
00:11:39,219 --> 00:11:42,291
These are measurements calculated by the
included training script,

202
00:11:42,358 --> 00:11:44,747
not illustrative numbers typed into the
slides.

203
00:11:44,815 --> 00:11:46,180
Those values need context.

204
00:11:46,248 --> 00:11:50,344
The synthetic event rate is only about
four and a half percent

205
00:11:50,411 --> 00:11:52,460
among rows eligible for early warning.

206
00:11:52,527 --> 00:11:56,623
At a threshold tuned toward recall, some
alerts will be false positives.

207
00:11:56,690 --> 00:12:00,787
Precision of about twenty-six percent
means that many positive model flags do

208
00:12:00,854 --> 00:12:02,902
not correspond to positive simulator
labels.

209
00:12:02,969 --> 00:12:07,065
The score does not prove that the same
ranking or alert trade-off

210
00:12:07,133 --> 00:12:08,839
would work in real patients.

211
00:12:08,907 --> 00:12:13,686
It only reports how well the classifier
learns patterns inside this particular data-generating process.

212
00:12:13,753 --> 00:12:17,508
For example, the confusion matrix in the
report records true negatives,

213
00:12:17,576 --> 00:12:21,330
false positives, false negatives and true
positives on the held-out group.

214
00:12:21,398 --> 00:12:24,129
The chosen threshold prioritises recall
more than precision,

215
00:12:24,196 --> 00:12:28,633
so the interface has to be careful about
the meaning of a flag.

216
00:12:28,701 --> 00:12:31,773
A flag is a prompt to review the record,

217
00:12:31,840 --> 00:12:34,230
not a reason to trigger an intervention.

218
00:12:34,298 --> 00:12:39,418
It is also important not to select the
model solely by its best reported metric.

219
00:12:39,485 --> 00:12:42,557
A real project would compare operational
costs, alert fatigue,

220
00:12:42,625 --> 00:12:46,379
calibration and clinical usefulness with intended
users before selecting a threshold.

221
00:12:46,447 --> 00:12:49,519
A real deployment would require external
validation, calibration analysis,

222
00:12:49,586 --> 00:12:52,658
comparison with appropriate baselines,
subgroup review and clinical oversight.

223
00:12:52,726 --> 00:12:56,822
We include the held-out predictions, metrics
file and global feature importance to

224
00:12:56,889 --> 00:13:01,285
support review and reproducibility rather than presenting
a single headline score without context.

225
00:13:01,327 --> 00:13:04,578
The dashboard is the main interaction
point for a reviewer.

226
00:13:04,645 --> 00:13:08,546
In the running Streamlit application, the
sidebar allows the reviewer to select

227
00:13:08,614 --> 00:13:12,516
a synthetic patient and advance the
timeline to a chosen hourly observation.

228
00:13:12,583 --> 00:13:15,509
The top row then displays the current
glucose-like reading,

229
00:13:15,576 --> 00:13:19,152
the two-hour model score, HbA one C and
other historical context,

230
00:13:19,219 --> 00:13:20,845
plus a wearable-style signal summary.

231
00:13:20,913 --> 00:13:24,489
The line chart presents the last
twenty-four hours of glucose-like values

232
00:13:24,556 --> 00:13:28,132
and marks the threshold used to create the
synthetic outcome label.

233
00:13:28,199 --> 00:13:31,776
The forecast panel converts the model
score into a review-oriented state.

234
00:13:31,844 --> 00:13:34,769
When the current reading is already above
the threshold,

235
00:13:34,837 --> 00:13:36,462
it displays that condition separately.

236
00:13:36,530 --> 00:13:40,431
If the review threshold is met, it asks
the reviewer to inspect

237
00:13:40,498 --> 00:13:45,050
signal quality and recent context; it does
not recommend a medication or treatment dose.

238
00:13:45,117 --> 00:13:49,018
The historical EHR snapshot gathers age,
body-mass index, duration, HbA one C,

239
00:13:49,086 --> 00:13:51,362
hypertension and simulated adherence in
one place.

240
00:13:51,429 --> 00:13:56,306
The feature-importance view gives a global summary of
what the fitted ensemble used during training.

241
00:13:56,373 --> 00:13:59,949
It is not a causal explanation for an
individual patient's score.

242
00:14:00,017 --> 00:14:03,918
During a live recording, this is the point
to interact with the

243
00:14:03,985 --> 00:14:08,212
patient selector, move the timeline, and
pause on the chart and EHR context.

244
00:14:08,279 --> 00:14:12,831
The distinction between the forecast and the
retrospective outcome is maintained in the application.

245
00:14:12,898 --> 00:14:16,149
The outcome expander is marked for
synthetic retrospective demonstration only

246
00:14:16,216 --> 00:14:20,118
because it is known only after the next
two hours have occurred

247
00:14:20,186 --> 00:14:21,485
in the simulated sequence.

248
00:14:21,553 --> 00:14:25,454
The forecast uses the current feature
vector and the model trained earlier.

249
00:14:25,521 --> 00:14:29,423
In a live demonstration, moving the slider
is a simple way to

250
00:14:29,491 --> 00:14:33,392
show that the twin changes state and the
model is re-run for

251
00:14:33,459 --> 00:14:37,318
a different observation rather than producing
one static score for every timestamp.

252
00:14:37,360 --> 00:14:41,071
The what-if panel enables a reviewer to
alter a few recent input

253
00:14:41,139 --> 00:14:44,849
values: carbohydrates logged in the last
hour, steps recorded in that hour

254
00:14:44,917 --> 00:14:47,082
and sleep duration from the previous
night.

255
00:14:47,149 --> 00:14:51,478
After applying the scenario, the model is
scored again using the altered feature vector.

256
00:14:51,546 --> 00:14:54,020
This creates a useful discussion about
model sensitivity:

257
00:14:54,087 --> 00:14:57,798
it shows whether the fitted model's output
changes when one or more

258
00:14:57,865 --> 00:14:59,411
of those input values changes.

259
00:14:59,479 --> 00:15:02,880
It is important to state what this panel
does not do.

260
00:15:02,948 --> 00:15:05,113
It is not a complete physiological
simulator.

261
00:15:05,180 --> 00:15:08,272
Changing the meal value does not model
digestion, hormonal response,

262
00:15:08,340 --> 00:15:12,978
insulin kinetics or all of the glucose
processes that would occur in a real body.

263
00:15:13,045 --> 00:15:16,756
The scenario is not a causal estimate and
does not prove that

264
00:15:16,824 --> 00:15:18,679
a particular action will reduce risk.

265
00:15:18,747 --> 00:15:23,385
It also does not recommend exercise, medication or
a dietary decision for a real person.

266
00:15:23,452 --> 00:15:27,163
We use it as a transparent software
interaction that helps reviewers see

267
00:15:27,231 --> 00:15:29,705
the relationship between inputs and a
fitted classifier.

268
00:15:29,772 --> 00:15:34,101
The dashboard labels the function as model-input
sensitivity analysis to avoid overstating its meaning.

269
00:15:34,169 --> 00:15:37,879
Future versions could use a physiologically
grounded simulator and work with clinicians

270
00:15:37,947 --> 00:15:40,730
to choose scenarios that are safe and
clinically meaningful.

271
00:15:40,797 --> 00:15:44,508
For a fair comparison, scenario testing
should start from a clearly defined

272
00:15:44,576 --> 00:15:47,050
baseline and adjust one factor at a time,

273
00:15:47,117 --> 00:15:48,972
while keeping all other assumptions
visible.

274
00:15:49,040 --> 00:15:52,751
If multiple factors change at once, it can
become hard to tell

275
00:15:52,819 --> 00:15:54,983
which change caused the score to move.

276
00:15:55,050 --> 00:15:58,761
Even one-factor sensitivity is not the
same as a causal intervention study,

277
00:15:58,828 --> 00:16:02,230
because the model is describing associations
it learned from synthetic trajectories.

278
00:16:02,297 --> 00:16:06,008
In this prototype, the panel is a way to
test the software

279
00:16:06,075 --> 00:16:09,786
interaction and explore model behaviour,
not to advise an individual on what

280
00:16:09,854 --> 00:16:11,667
to eat or how to exercise.

281
00:16:11,709 --> 00:16:15,213
The most important limitation is that both
the feature data

282
00:16:15,280 --> 00:16:17,383
and the outcome labels are synthetic.

283
00:16:17,450 --> 00:16:20,603
The simulator determines the patterns the
classifier can learn,

284
00:16:20,671 --> 00:16:24,174
so even a strong test score could reflect
simulator-specific assumptions

285
00:16:24,242 --> 00:16:27,045
rather than a pattern that generalises to
people.

286
00:16:27,113 --> 00:16:30,266
The model has not been evaluated
prospectively or externally.

287
00:16:30,333 --> 00:16:33,486
We have not established calibration for a
clinical population,

288
00:16:33,554 --> 00:16:37,057
evaluated performance across clinical subgroups,
or conducted clinician workflow studies.

289
00:16:37,125 --> 00:16:42,030
No live patient record, medical device or
external API is connected to the application.

290
00:16:42,097 --> 00:16:45,952
For those reasons, the prototype is not
intended for diagnosis, treatment,

291
00:16:46,020 --> 00:16:47,771
medication changes or emergency decisions.

292
00:16:47,838 --> 00:16:50,991
A low score is not a guarantee of safety,

293
00:16:51,059 --> 00:16:53,862
and a high score is not a diagnosis.

294
00:16:53,930 --> 00:16:57,433
A real system would need properly governed
and de-identified datasets,

295
00:16:57,501 --> 00:17:01,355
independent external validation, calibration, uncertainty estimates,
missing-data handling, subgroup performance checks,

296
00:17:01,422 --> 00:17:05,977
security review, audit logs, human-factors testing, clinical
governance and medical or regulatory assessment.

297
00:17:06,044 --> 00:17:09,197
The human reviewer remains in control in
this prototype.

298
00:17:09,265 --> 00:17:13,470
Privacy protection starts with the simple
fact that the repository does not

299
00:17:13,537 --> 00:17:14,938
contain real patient records.

300
00:17:15,006 --> 00:17:18,509
The limitations are documented in the
README and technical report

301
00:17:18,577 --> 00:17:22,782
because trust in a health-related AI
prototype depends on explaining what it

302
00:17:22,849 --> 00:17:26,353
cannot support as clearly as explaining
what it can do.

303
00:17:26,420 --> 00:17:28,172
Another limit is sensor quality.

304
00:17:28,239 --> 00:17:32,443
The synthetic series has complete hourly
rows and does not fully reflect

305
00:17:32,511 --> 00:17:36,366
communication gaps, clock drift, compression artefacts
or changes in device accuracy.

306
00:17:36,433 --> 00:17:41,338
Real-world data may be missing exactly
when a system needs to make a forecast.

307
00:17:41,406 --> 00:17:44,909
A safe implementation should flag
missingness, provide confidence or abstain

308
00:17:44,977 --> 00:17:48,480
when input quality is insufficient, and
allow clinicians to see

309
00:17:48,548 --> 00:17:50,649
which data contributed to the score.

310
00:17:50,717 --> 00:17:55,272
Security and governance would also become
central if real data were ever introduced.

311
00:17:55,339 --> 00:17:59,544
These issues are listed as future work
rather than being described as

312
00:17:59,612 --> 00:18:02,022
solved by the current proof of concept.

313
00:18:02,064 --> 00:18:05,777
The next phase would begin with data
quality and governance

314
00:18:05,845 --> 00:18:08,073
rather than immediately deploying a model.

315
00:18:08,140 --> 00:18:11,853
We would seek an appropriately approved,
de-identified and well-documented CGM

316
00:18:11,921 --> 00:18:16,006
and EHR dataset, define a realistic
clinical target with clinician input,

317
00:18:16,073 --> 00:18:19,044
and compare this baseline against
transparent alternative models.

318
00:18:19,112 --> 00:18:23,196
We would investigate missing sensors, uneven
sampling, calibration across risk groups

319
00:18:23,264 --> 00:18:25,120
and uncertainty around each prediction.

320
00:18:25,188 --> 00:18:29,644
We would also test whether the dashboard
fits a real clinical workflow

321
00:18:29,711 --> 00:18:35,281
and whether the wording of alerts creates a
useful review prompt rather than alert fatigue.

322
00:18:35,349 --> 00:18:39,805
GlucoTwin India demonstrates the complete challenge
loop within a narrow, testable scope:

323
00:18:39,873 --> 00:18:43,214
historical context, a dynamic sensor-like
stream, a two-hour estimate,

324
00:18:43,282 --> 00:18:45,510
retrospective measurement and a
human-in-the-loop dashboard.

325
00:18:45,577 --> 00:18:50,034
It does not claim clinical readiness or a
proven reduction in complications.

326
00:18:50,101 --> 00:18:53,814
Our goal is to show a reproducible
technical foundation on

327
00:18:53,882 --> 00:18:56,481
which careful further evaluation could be
built.

328
00:18:56,549 --> 00:18:58,776
Thank you for reviewing DigiTech's
submission.

329
00:18:58,843 --> 00:19:03,300
The public repository includes the source
code, trained model, synthetic dataset, tests,

330
00:19:03,367 --> 00:19:07,080
evaluation report, architecture diagram,
editable deck and this narration script.

331
00:19:07,148 --> 00:19:10,490
The remaining submission steps are to
verify the institution

332
00:19:10,558 --> 00:19:13,900
and registered team details, add the
public repository URL,

333
00:19:13,967 --> 00:19:16,153
and upload the required demonstration
video.

334
00:19:16,195 --> 00:19:19,283
The public-health context in this presentation
is sourced from the International Diabetes

335
00:19:19,350 --> 00:19:22,694
Federation's twenty twenty-five Diabetes Atlas and the
World Health Organization's diabetes fact sheet.

336
00:19:22,762 --> 00:19:26,106
The challenge requirements are taken from
the Digital Twin Challenge page on Unstop.

337
00:19:26,173 --> 00:19:29,260
Those references are included in the
README and on this final slide.

338
00:19:29,327 --> 00:19:31,900
The dataset and model metrics are
generated within the repository

339
00:19:31,967 --> 00:19:34,283
and should be interpreted only in the
synthetic domain.

340
00:19:34,351 --> 00:19:34,865
Thank you.

341
00:19:34,933 --> 00:19:37,248
The repository is intended to be easy to
reproduce:

342
00:19:37,315 --> 00:19:39,888
install the stated dependencies, generate
or load the synthetic file,

343
00:19:39,955 --> 00:19:42,013
train the baseline model and launch the
dashboard.

344
00:19:42,080 --> 00:19:45,168
The report records the seed, patient
counts, split sizes and test metrics.

345
00:19:45,235 --> 00:19:48,322
The source code and data dictionary
explain what each input means and

346
00:19:48,389 --> 00:19:50,447
which future-facing columns are excluded
from the model.

347
00:19:50,515 --> 00:19:52,830
We hope this makes the submission
straightforward to evaluate

348
00:19:52,898 --> 00:19:55,727
and provides a useful starting point for a
more rigorous study.

349
00:19:55,795 --> 00:19:56,267
Thank you.

